														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4094810">
									

<p>The short answers to any of the detail being incorrect are that (1, in-universe) the statement is not exact science by today's research on how we hear, but more along the lines of a belief of an expert character (also of an irl consult), and probably subject to debate among his colleagues and (2, out-of-universe) the present detail is sufficient enough for the article's purposes, and what isn't there is a willful handwave. But, I'm very interested in this kind of talk, so.</p>
<p>I don't know a whole lot about multilateration, and will read about it, but to add to this discussion from what's here already, this</p>
<blockquote>
<p>you can use the timing between when one microphone detects a spike and when another microphone detects a spike to figure out which one is further away from the source of the sound.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>is how the mammalian brain, specifically the inferior colliculus localizes sound (&quot;interaural cues&quot;).<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-970635-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-970635-1')">1</a></sup> It utilizes more minute cues than time and intensity discrepancies though; e.g. how the timbre of sound waves passing through our heads is modified by the time it hits the farther ear.</p>
<p>To stop short of a wall of text and to get to the bottom line of where I stand; an electrochemical and biological plexus that has been improved upon over millions of years by life-or-death forces is going to be superior in this case to the impressive advancement of mechanical technology we have achieved as a species, most of which is modeled after that very plexus.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-970635-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-970635-1')">1</a>. The mammalian hearing system does have the equivalent of a &quot;blind spot&quot;; a result of anatomy and a disadvantage microphone arrays wouldn't have. However, this is overcome in the same way a blind spot would be; just move your head a bit.</div>
</div>

